<article class="env-definition" id="04J2">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04J2">Definition <span data-tag="04J2">18.21.2</span>.</a> Let $(\mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C}), \mathcal{O})$ be a ringed topos. Let $\mathcal{F} \in \mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C})$. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
The ringed topos $(\mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C})/\mathcal{F}, \mathcal{O}_\mathcal {F})$ is called the <em>localization of the ringed topos $(\mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C}), \mathcal{O})$ at $\mathcal{F}$</em>. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The morphism of ringed topoi $(j_\mathcal {F}, j_\mathcal {F}^\sharp ) : (\mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C})/\mathcal{F}, \mathcal{O}_\mathcal {F}) \to (\mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C}), \mathcal{O})$ of Lemma <a href="/tag/04IE" data-tag="04IE">18.21.1</a> is called the <em>localization morphism</em>. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>